This paper develops the Yield Spread Suppression Theory (YSPS), which argues that sovereign yield differentials between trading nations create asymmetric finance costs through domestic banking channels. This cost constitutes a continuous structural trade barrier beyond tariffs and transport costs. It formalizes a four-stage transmission mechanism and introduces a non-linear SME exit threshold, where trade stops at the point when bilateral trade friction exceeds firm profit margins.&nbsp; 
<br>
 An illustrative case from Turkey's 2018 sovereign and currency crisis supports the central prediction. The paper extends the standard gravity model by appending a financial friction term and contributes to the literature on financial development and international trade.

Standard models of consumer choice represent utility as a scalar function of goods, abstracting from the possibility that utility may be generated through multiple distinct sources. This paper develops a compositional model of utility in which goods are characterized by their contributions to relief, comfort, status, and achievement, and individuals differ in the weights they assign to these components.&nbsp;
<div>
 The central contribution of the paper is to show that this compositional structure generates systematic patterns of sorting and market segmentation. Consumers select goods whose component structure aligns with their underlying preferences, leading to endogenous clustering of consumers across goods. As a result, markets may segment along compositional dimensions, with goods competing more directly within components than across them.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The model also yields novel implications for substitution. In contrast to standard formulations, substitution depends on the similarity of goods in their underlying component structure rather than solely on observable characteristics or total utility. Goods that differ in their dominant components may be weak substitutes even when they are similar in price or function, while goods that provide value through similar components may be close substitutes despite appearing different.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 These results provide a structural interpretation of heterogeneity in consumer behavior and extend standard demand theory by introducing a role for the sources of utility in shaping both individual choice and aggregate market outcomes. The compositional framework offers a tractable approach to integrating behavioral and economic perspectives in the analysis of consumer choice.
</div>
